      "snippet": "NumPy's std function computes the standard deviation along a specified axis, with a default of ddof=0 for the population standard deviation. The documentation notes that the standard deviation is the square root of the average of the squared deviations from the mean, and that `np.std(a) / np.mean(a)` is a common way to compute the coefficient of variation.",
      "domain": "numpy.org",
      "rank": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "numpy.mean \u2014 NumPy v2.1 Manual",
      "url": "https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.mean.html",
      "snippet": "`numpy.mean` returns the arithmetic mean over the requested axis, and examples in the docs show its use on 1-D and multi-dimensional arrays. The function accepts an `axis` argument and can preserve dimensions with `keepdims=True`, which is often paired with `np.std` when calculating normalized statistics like the coefficient of variation.",
      "domain": "numpy.org",
      "rank": 2
    },
    {
      "title": "Coefficient of Variation Calculator with Python NumPy Example",
      "url": "https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/coefficient-of-variation-in-python/",
      "snippet": "A common Python formula for coefficient of variation is `cv = np.std(data) / np.mean(data)`; multiplying by 100 gives a percentage. The article explains that NumPy's default standard deviation uses `ddof=0`, which matches the population standard deviation formula used in many introductory statistics examples.",
      "domain": "geeksforgeeks.org",
      "rank": 3
    },
    {
      "title": "Python Statistics: Mean, Standard Deviation, and Coefficient of Variation",
      "url": "https://www.statology.org/coefficient-of-variation-python/",
      "snippet": "Statology shows how to calculate coefficient of variation in Python with NumPy using `np.std(x) / np.mean(x)` and then converting to a percentage by multiplying by 100. The example highlights the population standard deviation setting (`ddof=0`) and compares it with sample standard deviation, where `ddof=1` is used.",
      "domain": "statology.org",
      "rank": 4
    },
    {
      "title": "How to Calculate the Coefficient of Variation in Python",
      "url": "https://datagy.io/coefficient-of-variation-python/",
      "snippet": "This guide demonstrates that the coefficient of variation is the ratio of the standard deviation to the mean, commonly expressed as `CV = \u03c3 / \u03bc` or `np.std(values) / np.mean(values)`. It also notes that for population data, NumPy's default `ddof=0` should be used, while `ddof=1` produces the sample standard deviation.",
      "domain": "datagy.io",
      "rank": 5
    }
  ],
  "query": "Python numpy mean population standard deviation coefficient of variation formula",
  "count": 5
}

